<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Shared build and packaging properties live in src/Directory.Build.props. Only what differs
    between the two packages belongs here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageId>Ank.DocToolkit</PackageId>
    <!--
      THE FIRST THING A CONSUMER READS on nuget.org, and nothing checks it. This text described a
      smaller library than shipped for four releases - it named only HTML to DOCX/PDF while
      Markdown import, the DOCX text exporters, XLSX export and PDF text extraction had all
      shipped. Exactly the failure that made docfx/guides/capabilities.md a GENERATED page, one
      surface earlier in a consumer's journey.

      Re-read this whenever a converter is added. `docfx/guides/capabilities.md` is the derived
      list to check it against - and check it PAIR BY PAIR, not by skimming for format names.

      That distinction is the second failure this text has had, found 2026-08-15 while working the
      Release-PR curation checklist on 0.27.3. Every format was named, so nothing looked wrong; but
      three of the eleven converters in the matrix - DOCX, XLSX and PPTX to PDF - appeared nowhere
      in the sentence. The first failure was an omission of whole capabilities and was obvious once
      read; this one was invisible to any reading that asked "is DOCX mentioned?" instead of "is
      DOCX to PDF mentioned?".

      D20's nuspec check proves this field is NON-EMPTY. Nothing can prove it is COMPLETE, which is
      why the checklist item stayed human - and why it took a person working that list to find it.
    -->
    <!--
      REORDERED 2026-08-23, NOT REWRITTEN, and the distinction is the point. F7 measured this
      package at rank >50 on nuget.org for every query it exists to answer, including
      `html to pdf without browser` - which returns ONE result across all of nuget.org, and it is
      not this package. nuget.org searches the description, and the winning package leads with the
      constraint; this one buried "no native binaries, no browser" in its FOURTH line behind a
      60-word capability list.

      So the first sentence now leads with the job and the constraint, and the inventory follows.
      ALL TWELVE CONVERSION PAIRS ARE STILL NAMED - re-checked pair by pair against
      docfx/guides/capabilities.md when this was reordered, which is the check the two failures
      above exist to enforce. Do not trade completeness for brevity here: an omission is the
      failure this field has already had twice, and the second one was invisible to any reading
      that asked "is DOCX mentioned?" rather than "is DOCX to PDF mentioned?".
    -->
    <Description>
      Convert HTML to PDF and DOCX in C# without a browser - pure managed, no Chromium, no
      wkhtmltopdf, no native binaries, no LibreOffice, no Office interop. Also converts Markdown to
      DOCX and PDF; renders DOCX, XLSX and PPTX to PDF; exports DOCX to HTML/Markdown and XLSX to
      CSV/HTML; reads legacy Word 97-2003 .doc files; reads text out of a PDF; creates/edits DOCX,
      XLSX and PPTX; password-protects PDF, DOCX, XLSX and PPTX; and inspects and validates digital
      signatures in DOCX, XLSX and PPTX. Runs on Linux, Windows, macOS and arm64, and makes no
      network calls at runtime.
    </Description>
    <!--
      Three groups, and the ORDER of thought matters more than the list. The old set was eight
      format nouns - docx;pdf;xlsx;pptx;html;openxml;document;conversion - which describe what the
      package is to somebody who already found it. Nobody in pain searches "openxml".

        1. Formats, kept: people do search these.
        2. The words normal people use (word, excel, powerpoint) and THE JOB they are doing
           (html-to-docx, docx-to-pdf, mail-merge) - the symptom that brought them here.
        3. The four constraints (linux, cross-platform, offline). These ARE the differentiator and
           not one of them was a tag.

      Dropped `document` and `conversion` as too generic to rank for, and `html` in favour of the
      more specific `html-to-docx`.

      DELIBERATELY NOT KEYWORD-STUFFED. nuget.org dilutes relevance across a package's tags, so
      twenty weak tags rank worse than ten strong ones. Adding a tag has a cost; keep this list
      short and argue for anything new.

      TEN ADDED 2026-08-23 (F7), which stretches that last rule, so here is the argument.

      The rule above is an ASSERTION, not a measurement - nothing here has ever measured dilution.
      What F7 did measure is that this package ranks >50 for every query it exists to answer, and
      that `html to pdf`, the capability the package LEADS WITH, was not a tag at all. It was a
      GitHub topic and not a NuGet tag, which is the exact surface mismatch F7 was filed about.

        - html-to-pdf, html2pdf   the flagship capability, and the spelling people actually type.
        - pdf-generation          a 1,879-hit query this package answers.
        - document-conversion     a 310-hit query, and already a GitHub topic - the two surfaces
                                  disagreed, which is the whole of F7.
        - browserless, no-chromium, no-native-binaries, air-gapped
                                  the CONSTRAINT vocabulary. This is the winnable ground: the head
                                  terms belong to libraries with tens of millions of downloads and
                                  cannot be taken with metadata, while `html to pdf without
                                  browser` returns ONE result across all of nuget.org. Every one of
                                  these four is enforced by a premise guard, so none is a claim the
                                  package cannot support - the same rule D32 set for README SEO.
        - csharp, dotnet          NOT for their own sake, which would be hopeless. NuGet ranks
                                  higher when metadata contains ALL of a query's terms, and
                                  `generate pdf c#` was one of the measured failures.

      TWO OF F7's PROPOSED TAGS WERE DELIBERATELY HELD BACK, to limit exactly the dilution the rule
      above warns about: `pure-managed` (a synonym of no-native-binaries, no measured query) and
      `document-generation` (a near-duplicate of document-conversion, no measured query).

      THIS IS UNVERIFIED UNTIL RE-MEASURED. F7 step 4 is to re-run the same queries once the next
      release reaches the index, which lags one to two days, and record before/after in the ticket.
      Tags are necessary and not sufficient: this package already carried `html-to-docx` and still
      does not reach the top 20 for `html to docx`, because download count weighs heavily. If the
      re-measurement shows no movement, the honest response is to cut back, not to add more.

      FIFTEEN MORE ADDED 2026-08-31 (F12, F13) - the same rule, tested against new evidence rather
      than skipped.

      F12: a hyphenated tag is stored and matched as ONE token, by PREFIX only. Measured by pairing
      each candidate word with an anchor this package already owns (`browserless`, 6 hits;
      `doctoolkit`, 4 hits - same result from both, which is what makes it a property of the index
      rather than of one anchor): `merge` does not match `mail-merge`, only `mail` does;
      `conversion` does not match `document-conversion`, only `document` does; and so on for every
      hyphenated tag tried. A positive control rules out "hyphenated queries are just broken":
      `serilog structured-logging` finds Serilog, because ITS second word lives in prose, not
      welded into one tag. So the fix is additive - keep the whole compound for an exact-phrase
      searcher, add the second half as its OWN tag:

        - conversion, generation, merge   second halves of document-conversion, pdf-generation,
                                           mail-merge - concepts already committed to, now
                                           reachable by either word.
        - platform, chromium, binaries    second halves of cross-platform, no-chromium,
                                           no-native-binaries - the constraint vocabulary, same
                                           reasoning.

      Measured and DELIBERATELY NOT added: `browser`, `managed`, `pure`, `injection` all already
      match individually via the DESCRIPTION (which tokenises on hyphens, unlike the tags field),
      and none has a specific query gap tied to it - `browser` is F7's own `html to pdf without
      browser` case, which that ticket already traced to how nuget.org conjoins `html` with
      `browser` and said explicitly not to chase with more tags.

      F13: a second, different failure - a query naming a real shipped capability, returning a
      HANDFUL of results, matching none of them. Ranking cannot be the obstacle when the total hit
      count is 2-30; this is a case where the word is simply ABSENT from what nuget.org indexes for
      this package, or - the more surprising half, confirmed by testing `markdown`, `csv`,
      `protect` and `digital` individually - present only in prose and too weakly weighted to
      survive an AND across two or three terms. Nine words, one per measured miss-query rather than
      a sweep of every capability this package has:

        - template            `fill word template`, 3 hits            -> DocxMailMerge.Merge
        - footnotes            `footnotes word`, 8 hits                -> DocxEditor.AddFootnote
        - revisions            `track changes word`, 2 hits            -> DocxReview.AcceptRevisions
        - formatting           `excel conditional formatting`, 17 hits -> XlsxRule
        - validation           `excel data validation`, 59 hits        -> XlsxValidation
        - markdown, csv, protect, digital-signature
                               already matched via prose alone (measured individually) and the
                               combined query STILL missed - `docx to markdown` (27), `xlsx to csv`
                               (20), `password protect docx` (4), `digital signature docx` (12) -
                               so these move from implied-by-prose to an explicit tag, the same
                               move F7 made for `browserless`.

      NOT chased with the whole no-match list - `editor`, `chart`, `pivot`, `table`, `form`,
      `comment`, `image`, `slide`, `spreadsheet`, `presentation` and others all measured absent too,
      but none is tied to a specific miss-query in the 92-query file, and F13 says explicitly not to
      sweep them in on spec.

      `pptx to pdf` (30 hits) is a genuine open miss with NO good candidate tag: `pptx` and `pdf`
      are both already present as strong tags, so the failure is unexplained rather than fixed here.

      Both F12 and F13 are folded into ONE release rather than two, because publishing is the
      maintainer's call and re-measuring needs a real release either way - splitting them would
      spend two release cycles on two diagnoses from the same research pass. THIS WHOLE BLOCK IS
      UNVERIFIED UNTIL RE-MEASURED, same as the block above: re-run
      artifacts/discoverability/nuget-rank.py once the next release indexes, and cut back rather
      than add more if the F13 words show no movement.
    -->
    <PackageTags>docx;pdf;xlsx;pptx;word;excel;powerpoint;openxml;html-to-docx;html-to-pdf;html2pdf;docx-to-pdf;pdf-generation;document-conversion;mail-merge;browserless;no-chromium;no-native-binaries;air-gapped;linux;cross-platform;offline;csharp;dotnet;conversion;generation;merge;platform;chromium;binaries;template;footnotes;revisions;formatting;validation;markdown;csv;protect;digital-signature</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>


  <!--
    README.md, THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt and the shared icon are packed by
    src/Directory.Build.props. LICENSE is packed by this project only, matching what has shipped
    since 0.1.0 - the extensions package has never carried one. That asymmetry is preserved here
    rather than quietly fixed, because changing a package's contents is a release-visible change
    and belongs in its own commit.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    TableRowFinder is internal - consumers use DocxEditor, never it directly - but its one
    dangerous behaviour, refusing to reach into nested tables, fails SILENTLY when it regresses:
    the document is still schema-valid, just wrong. Proving that end-to-end shows a bad document
    without showing which rows were chosen, so the test project gets direct access.
    Same trade the extensions package already makes for its internal service implementations.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DocToolkit.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Marks this assembly safe to trim. This attribute IS the claim: it is exactly what the MSBuild
      <IsTrimmable> property emits, and what the trimmer reads to decide an assembly's default
      action.

      Written as the attribute rather than the property on purpose, and the reason is not style.
      Setting <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable> makes the SDK inject a Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks
      PackageReference at the version bundled with THAT SDK: 8.0.22/10.0.1 on the machine this was
      written, 8.0.29/10.0.10 on the CI runner. Both projects commit a packages.lock.json that CI
      asserts in locked mode, so that entry breaks the build on any SDK patch difference between any
      two machines, permanently. Measured: it failed CI on the first push, NU1004 "the package
      reference Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks version has changed from [10.0.1, ) to [10.0.10, )".

      It also has to live in the project file rather than src/Directory.Build.props. An
      AssemblyMetadata item declared there makes multi-target framework inference fail outright with
      NETSDK1013, "the TargetFramework value was not recognized" - measured, and a Condition on
      TargetFramework does not avoid it.

      What this costs: the trim ANALYZER no longer runs over src/ at build time, since it ships in
      that package. What replaces it is strictly stronger. ci.yml's trim-guard job trim-publishes
      tests/TrimProbe over the whole dependency closure, fails on any warning naming DocToolkit, and
      RUNS the trimmed binary. The analyzer only ever saw our own source; the guard also sees
      ClosedXML, OpenXML, HtmlToOpenXml and OfficeIMO. Mutation-verified: an unsafe reflection call
      added to PptxSlide.Titled fails that job.

      IsAotCompatible is set as an attribute too, for the same two reasons as IsTrimmable above -
      the property would pull in Microsoft.NET.ILLink.Tasks and break locked-mode restore across
      SDK patches, and an AssemblyMetadata item in src/Directory.Build.props fails framework
      inference. (Written "locked-mode restore" rather than with the flag's leading double hyphen,
      which XML forbids inside a comment - it failed the build once already.)

      IT IS SET ONLY BECAUSE IT IS NOW VERIFIED, 2026-08-14. This used to read "deliberately not
      set: nothing has verified it end to end, and an unverified compatibility claim is worse than
      an absent one" - which was the right call for as long as it was true. What changed is
      evidence, not opinion: ci.yml's aot-guard job native-AOT-publishes tests/AotProbe over the
      real dependency closure and RUNS it, and every capability asserts on its result rather than
      merely completing. Measured on the first green run, on main:

        docx ok (1979 bytes) | replace ok | pptx ok (4945) | xlsx ok (6280)
        html->docx ok (1951) | docx->pdf ok (52850) | pdf->text ok

      including PdfPig's CMap handling and OfficeIMO's font work, which are the two most
      reflection-dependent paths in the graph. The only AOT warning in the whole closure is
      ClosedXML's IL2090; none is attributable to DocToolkit, which aot-guard asserts separately.

      A publish that merely LINKS would not have earned this. AOT breaks at runtime, when a type
      resolved by name turns out not to be there - so "it ran and the output was correct" is the
      only evidence worth anything here. If aot-guard ever goes red, remove this attribute rather
      than weakening the job.
    -->
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="IsTrimmable" Value="True" />
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="IsAotCompatible" Value="True" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE SPLIT: this is the only packable project, and the six beside it are packed INTO this
    package rather than published alongside it. The library ships as ONE package - see
    docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-23-format-project-split-design.md for why a multi-PACKAGE split
    was measured and rejected.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput);IncludeReferencedProjectInPackage</TargetsForTfmSpecificBuildOutput>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      PrivateAssets="all" stops each of these becoming a <dependency> on a package id that does not
      exist on nuget.org.

      It ALSO suppresses their own PackageReferences from reaching the nuspec, which is why every
      package this library needs is declared in src/Directory.Build.props instead. Measured
      2026-08-23: with OfficeIMO.Word declared only in the Docx project, the produced nuspec had NO
      <dependency> entries at all while still shipping the assembly that needs it - a package that
      installs cleanly, restores cleanly, and throws TypeLoadException on the consumer's machine.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DocToolkit.Primitives\DocToolkit.Primitives.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DocToolkit.Docx\DocToolkit.Docx.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DocToolkit.Xlsx\DocToolkit.Xlsx.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DocToolkit.Pptx\DocToolkit.Pptx.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DocToolkit.Pdf\DocToolkit.Pdf.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DocToolkit.Html\DocToolkit.Html.csproj" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THIS TARGET CANNOT BE PACKED WITH THE `no-build` SWITCH, and 0.36.0 shipped broken because it
    was. `release.yml` must run a full `dotnet pack`.

    This target reads @(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths), which only ResolveReferences populates.
    `release.yml` packs with `dotnet pack` and the `no-build` switch, which does NOT run it - so the item list was
    empty, BuildOutputInPackage got nothing, and the package went to nuget.org carrying
    DocToolkit.dll ALONE: a 67 KB shell with the other six assemblies absent, against 266 KB of
    working library in pre-split 0.35.0. Every consumer touching DocxEditor, PageSetup, PdfEditor,
    WorkbookEditor or PresentationEditor got CS0012/CS0103 and could not compile.

    It passed every check because nothing packed the way the release packs. A plain `dotnet pack`
    runs ResolveReferences and produces all seven assemblies, so every local pack was correct and
    only the release was wrong. Measured 2026-08-24: plain pack seven assemblies, clean-tree pack
    seven, `no-build` pack ONE.

    Adding DependsOnTargets="ResolveReferences" here does NOT fix it - NoBuild forbids the build
    that ResolveReferences then wants, so pack fails outright with NETSDK1085. The switch has to go
    from the workflow, which is what happened.

    scripts/check-package-contents.py asserts the assembly list inside the built nupkg, on the
    publish path, so this cannot ship a second time.
  -->
  <Target Name="IncludeReferencedProjectInPackage">
    <ItemGroup>
      <BuildOutputInPackage Include="@(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths->WithMetadataValue('ReferenceSourceTarget','ProjectReference'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
